================================================================
EVALUATION CORPUS -- TOOL DISTRIBUTION ANALYSIS (2026-09-09)
================================================================
HARD BLOCKER FOUND: MCPCorpus CANNOT give us tool-level distribution.
  Of 13,875 records, only 55 parse to >=1 tool schema (92 tools total).
  185 records have a non-empty "tools" field but 130 of those are crawler
  junk (literal values like '$2d', '$2b' -- unresolved React server-component
  refs). Within our 1,847 ToFu-runnable repos, only 11 have usable schemas.
  => Any tool-level distribution MUST come from extracting tools ourselves.

WHAT COVERAGE ACTUALLY MEANS (read from src/sanitizers/):
  21 runtime tracer classes in introspection.py, spanning 52 distinct CWEs:
    FileAccess, FileWrite, Deserialization, CommandExecution, SQLExecution,
    CodeExecution, NetworkExecution, TempFileLeak, LogInjection,
    SensitiveDataLogging, SSLCertValidation, UnrestrictedIPBinding, ReDoS,
    BlockingEventLoopCall, WeakHash, WeakPRNG, NonceReuse,
    UnverifiedCodeDownload, XXE, DataAmplification, LoopCondition
  Stratify on THESE, not on mcp.so's marketing categories.

mcp.so CATEGORIES ARE USELESS FOR STRATIFICATION:
  developer-tools 1008 + research-and-data 576 = 85% of the pool.
  23 categories total, most with <20 repos.

SINK-FAMILY PROXY (keyword on name/title/description/category, 1847 repos):
  NetworkExec/SSRF 351 (19.0%) | CommandExecution 208 (11.3%)
  SQLExecution 117 (6.3%) | FileAccess/Write 103 (5.6%)
  UnverifiedDownload 60 | SensitiveDataLog 36 | CodeExecution 31
  DataAmplification 29 | SSLCert 26 | WeakHash/PRNG 19 | TempFileLeak 18
  BlockingEventLoop 5 | XXE 3 | ReDoS 3 | Deserialization 1
  (IPBinding matched 84.7% -- BROKEN PROBE, "server|port|host" matches nearly
   every MCP description. Treat as a measurement artifact, not a finding.)
  198 repos match zero families.

*** THE CENTRAL TENSION -- needs a decision ***
  "uniform distribution across languages/activity" and "hit all 66 sanitizers"
  are INCOMPATIBLE at n=25. Deserialization has 1 candidate repo, XXE 3,
  ReDoS 3, BlockingEventLoop 5. A uniform random draw of 25 from 1,847 will
  hit NONE of them with high probability.
  Resolution used by real benchmark papers (incl. VIPER-MCP): report TWO sets
  and never conflate them --
    (1) a random stratified set  -> supports ecosystem/representativeness claims
    (2) a purposive targeted set -> supports capability/coverage claims
  VIPER-MCP does exactly this: 39,884 mined repos for breadth, plus a separate
  130-vulnerable + 130-benign hand-built benchmark for measured FPR/FNR.

NEXT STEP (agreed plan): two-stage extraction.
  Stage 1 (done): cheap proxy shortlisting from corpus metadata.
  Stage 2 (todo): shallow-clone ~150-250 shortlisted repos and PARSE real tool
    registrations -- Python: @mcp.tool / @server.tool / types.Tool( /
    @app.list_tools ; Node/TS: server.tool( / setRequestHandler(
    ListToolsRequestSchema / tools:[ -- then compute the TRUE distribution of
    tools per repo and argument semantics per tool, and only then pick the 25.

