================================================================
COVERAGE ON JS TARGETS IS INERT -- INVESTIGATION, 2026-09-10
(reported only, NO code changed; this is Pierre's A6 and it needs your call)
================================================================

FINDING: on Node/TS targets, libFuzzer's cov: never changes during a run.
Coverage guidance is therefore dead and ToFu degenerates to random mutation
of a 2-3 entry corpus. Node/TS is 1,039 of the 1,847 runnable repos, and
Set A is majority node, so this affects most of the evaluation.

EVIDENCE (all from real logs, nothing inferred)
  excel-reader-mcp::filter_rows, 60s budget:
      #14  INITED cov: 3768 ft: 5631 corp: 3/263b exec/s: 0
      #364 REDUCE cov: 3768 ft: 5631 corp: 3/121b exec/s: 30
  mcp-appium-gestures::generate-tap-code, 600s budget:
      #4   pulse  cov: 2766 ft: 3768 corp: 2/153b exec/s: 0
      #309 REDUCE cov: 2766 ft: 4805 corp: 4/93b  exec/s: 0
  cov: is IDENTICAL from the first status line to the last, over 600 seconds.
  Both excel tools report the same 3768 -- it is a property of the SERVER,
  sampled once, not a measurement of what the fuzzer explored.
  metrics/coverage_timeline_20260909_220315.csv is 62 rows: 60 of them
  cov_edges=3768, 2 of them 0. That is the data behind any coverage figure.

MECHANISM (four links, all confirmed in source)
  1. src/runners/coverage_wrapper.js:23-24 calls
     Profiler.startPreciseCoverage ONCE at startup, then line 117 calls
     Profiler.takePreciseCoverage per request WITHOUT stopping/restarting.
     V8 precise-coverage counters are CUMULATIVE, so every call returns the
     whole process's coverage so far, not the delta for that call.
  2. src/runners/js_runner.py:34 initialises `self.already_covered = set()`
     -- and NEVER USES IT AGAIN. That dead attribute is the missing delta
     computation. The intended code is obviously
     `new = coverage_set - self.already_covered` before mapping.
  3. So after execution #1 the mapped index set is fixed; js_runner.py:176-180
     assigns no new cov_map entries, and fake_coverage.trigger() reaches no
     new branch. cov: is pinned for the rest of the run.
  4. libFuzzer therefore sees NO new coverage from ANY input, so no input is
     ever "interesting", so the corpus cannot grow: corp: stays at 2-4 despite
     LLM_SEEDGEN_OK n_seeds=13. Coverage-guided fuzzing is not happening.

SECOND, LATENT DEFECT (not currently binding, would bite once #3 is fixed)
  js_runner.py:178  self.next_cov_idx = (self.next_cov_idx + 1) % 10000
  but fake_coverage.trigger() only has branches for idx 0..1999 (2000 ifs,
  then `return -1`). Any index >= 2000 falls through the entire chain to the
  same terminal path, so distinct JS coverage items beyond the 2000th become
  indistinguishable AND each such call executes all 2000 comparisons. Either
  raise the generated chain to 10000 or take the modulo at 2000 -- but note
  that an index space smaller than the target's coverage surface aliases
  distinct edges onto one slot either way, which is its own bias.

THIRD OBSERVATION -- THROUGHPUT
  exec/s runs 0-33. The appium 600s run completed ~309 executions total.
  A coverage-guided fuzzer doing hundreds of executions per run is not
  meaningfully exploring anything, regardless of the coverage bug. The cost is
  a JSON round-trip per execution over a Unix socket plus a full
  takePreciseCoverage on every call (which gets more expensive as cumulative
  coverage grows -- fixing link 1 above should also speed this up).

WHAT THIS MEANS FOR THE PAPER (A6, and RQ4)
  - Any coverage-over-time figure for a JS target is FLAT BY CONSTRUCTION.
    The "coverage plateaus in under an hour" claim Pierre flagged is not
    merely unsupported by a 24h-only figure -- on JS targets it plateaus at
    t=0, as an artifact. Do not ship that claim from JS data.
  - Python targets are NOT affected by this bug: fuzzer.py:909 instruments the
    target's own modules directly via atheris.instrument_imports, so cov: is
    real there. That makes the metric NON-COMPARABLE across languages, which
    is exactly the caveat A6 asks for -- now with a mechanism to cite.
  - RQ4 has no coverage evidence on the majority of the corpus until link 2
    is fixed and the JS runs are redone.

- TO DOUBLE-CHECK (Ananto, do not erase until confirmed done):
    8. Decide whether to fix js_runner (wire up `already_covered` to send only
       the per-call delta) and re-run the JS targets, or to scope every
       coverage claim in the paper to Python targets and say plainly that the
       JS runner reports a cumulative snapshot rather than per-input coverage.
    9. If fixing: also settle the index-space question in the second defect
       above, then re-check exec/s -- the throughput number may change a lot.
   10. Confirm independently that V8's takePreciseCoverage is cumulative
       without stopPreciseCoverage (I read this off the wrapper's usage plus
       the flat cov: line, not off the CDP spec).

================================================================
DETERMINISM + JS COVERAGE FIXES -- 2026-09-10 (both applied)
================================================================

FIX 1: THE LLM WAS SAMPLING AT TEMPERATURE 0.7
  src/llm.py built ChatOpenAI(api_key, base_url, model, timeout, max_retries)
  with NO temperature and NO seed, so it inherited LangChain's default of 0.7.
  Every sanitizer selection was a sample from a distribution.
  Consequences, all of which invalidate results already discussed:
    - No run was reproducible. The paper cannot claim reproducibility.
    - The measured selection-precision failures (markitdown checks for an
      Appium gesture tool) are NOT attributable to llama3.1:8b being weak --
      they could be single unlucky draws. That evidence is now suspect and
      should be re-collected under temperature=0.
    - Any model-vs-model comparison would have measured sampling noise as much
      as the model, which would have quietly broken the RQ4-style ablation.
  NOW: TOFU_LLM_TEMPERATURE (default 0) and TOFU_LLM_SEED (default 20260910),
  both recorded in every run record's provenance. Seed is passed as the named
  ChatOpenAI arg with a model_kwargs fallback for older langchain-openai.
  CAVEAT written into the code: temperature=0 is necessary but not sufficient
  for bit-identical output -- llama.cpp/Ollama batching and GPU scheduling can
  still perturb logits. Run one tool twice and diff the selections before
  claiming reproducibility.

FIX 2: JS COVERAGE IS NOW PER-CALL, SO GUIDANCE ACTUALLY WORKS
  src/runners/coverage_wrapper.js reported every V8 range with count > 0 against
  counters that are cumulative since startPreciseCoverage, so the coverage array
  was identical on every call after warm-up (see the previous section for the
  full evidence chain).
  NOW: a module-level prevCounts map keyed on url|startOffset|endOffset, and a
  range is reported only when its count has INCREASED since the last take --
  i.e. it was executed by THIS call. callCount:true was already being requested,
  so no new profiler capability was needed.
  src/runners/js_runner.py correspondingly triggers a slot for every item in the
  per-call set on every execution (the mapping item -> slot stays stable for the
  runner's life), because libFuzzer requires the edge set to be a function of
  the input. `already_covered` is now maintained for reporting only.

  Also fixed the index-space mismatch: indices were assigned `% 10000` against a
  generated chain of only 2000 branches, so every item past the 2000th fell
  through to the same `return -1`. COV_SLOTS (env TOFU_JS_COV_SLOTS, default
  10000) now drives BOTH, fake_coverage.py is regenerated whenever its branch
  count no longer matches, and exhausting the space prints a loud one-time
  warning and aliases by hash instead of collapsing everything onto one path.
  js_cov_slots is recorded in run provenance.

  PREDICTION TO VERIFY ON THE NEXT JS RUN (this is the acceptance test):
    (a) cov: must now INCREASE during a run instead of being pinned.
    (b) corp: must grow past 2-4 entries.
    (c) exec/s should rise substantially. The old code called
        fake_coverage.trigger() once per item in the CUMULATIVE set on EVERY
        execution -- roughly 1400-1900 calls each walking an average of ~700-950
        if-comparisons, so order 1-2 million comparisons per execution. That is
        why appium logged exec/s: 0 and completed ~309 executions in 600s.
    If (a) does not happen, the remaining suspect is worker-thread coverage:
    coverage_wrapper.js attaches sessions to Node Workers (lines 27-41) but
    `combinedResults` only ever collects from the MAIN session, so any target
    doing its work in a worker reports nothing. NOT FIXED -- no target in Set
    A/B is known to use workers. Check before blaming the delta logic.

- TO DOUBLE-CHECK (Ananto, do not erase until confirmed done):
   11. Run one JS tool and confirm (a), (b), (c) above before launching any
       campaign whose coverage numbers you intend to publish.
   12. Re-collect the sanitizer-selection precision evidence under
       temperature=0. The earlier markitdown/SSRF misselections were sampled at
       0.7 and cannot be attributed to the model as they stand.
   13. Decide the model tier (see below) and record the exact model + endpoint
       + temperature + seed in the experimental-setup subsection (A3).

MODEL CHOICE -- decided 2026-09-10: gemma4:12b as the local mid arm (16 GB M2 Pro)
  llama3.1:8b dates to mid-2024 and is two generations old; Ollama's current
  tools-capable library is qwen3.8/qwen3.6 (27B/35B), gemma4 (12B/26B/31B),
  granite4.1 (3B/8B/30B), glm-5.x, nemotron3 and minimax-m3. On 16 GB unified
  memory the 27B+ class does not fit alongside a running target server and
  ToFu itself (~16 GB at q4 for a 27B, and MoE models need ALL weights resident
  regardless of active-parameter count, so nemotron-3.5-lightning's 30B total
  rules it out too). gemma4:12b is roughly 7 GB at q4 and leaves headroom.
  Proposed arms, all at temperature=0 and the same seed:
    - llama3.1:8b   keep as the OLD baseline, for continuity with what exists
    - gemma4:12b    the local mid arm
    - granite4.1:8b optional third: IBM tunes Granite for tool calling, which
                    is the role llama3.1:8b visibly struggled with (seed gen
                    needed attempt=2 on get_cell and fell back on export_to_json)
  A hosted frontier arm is DEFERRED until the local sweep is in; if added, note
  in sec10_ethical that third-party tool descriptions would leave the machine.
  DO NOT report model rankings from secondary sources -- the search results for
  local-model comparisons are SEO content farms with no primary data, and BFCL's
  leaderboard did not render. Any ranking in the paper must come from our own
  measurements on our own labeled set.

  OPERATIONAL CAVEAT: run_eval defaults to --workers 4 and all four workers hit
  the same Ollama daemon at run start (2 calls each: selection + seed gen). With
  a 12B on 16 GB that will contend. Set OLLAMA_NUM_PARALLEL=1 and consider
  --workers 2 for the first gemma4 campaign, then compare wall-clock. Part of
  the fixed ~90-140s per-run overhead visible in the 60s-budget smoke run
  (150.2s and 200.9s actual) is very likely this contention.

STILL NEEDED BEFORE RQ2/RQ3 SAY ANYTHING: a labeled ground truth for sanitizer
selection. Take 30-50 tools across Set A/B, label which sanitizer families
genuinely apply to each, then measure precision/recall PER MODEL. Without it
"selection precision" is anecdote. This is cheap and is itself a contribution.

