================================================================
JS COVERAGE -- THE ORIGINAL DIAGNOSIS WAS WRONG. 2026-09-10 08:20
Both prior conclusions overturned by direct measurement.
================================================================

item 10 in this file asked someone to confirm, independently, that
takePreciseCoverage is cumulative without stopPreciseCoverage, because that was
read off the wrapper's usage and a flat cov: line rather than off the CDP spec.
It is NOT cumulative. Measured with a standalone inspector.Session, same
1000-iteration loop before each of three consecutive takes:
    take #1: nonzero=8 maxCount=1000
    take #2: nonzero=8 maxCount=1000
    take #3: nonzero=8 maxCount=1000
Counts do not accumulate; takePreciseCoverage RESETS the execution counters.
range.count was ALREADY "executed by this call". Item 10 is answered: NO.

=> FIX 2 (the prevCounts delta) was built on a false premise and made things
   strictly worse. It stored the max count seen and reported only
   count > prevCount, so after the first call almost every range was
   suppressed. REVERTED.

=> BUT THE REVERT DID NOT RESTORE COVERAGE GUIDANCE EITHER, which means the
   ORIGINAL pinned-cov diagnosis in "COVERAGE ON JS TARGETS IS INERT" was also
   wrong about the mechanism. The real cause, measured on one function with
   three branches, detailed:true:
       [routes  ] isBlockCoverage=true ranges= 157-265:1  207-263:0
       [services] isBlockCoverage=true ranges= 157-265:1  194-207:0  244-263:0
       [zzz     ] isBlockCoverage=true ranges= 157-265:1  194-207:0  231-244:0
   V8 emits ONE range spanning the whole function carrying its execution count,
   then carves the branches that did NOT run out of it as nested count:0
   sub-ranges. So the set of count>0 ranges is IDENTICAL (just 157-265) for all
   three inputs, and every bit of branch information lives in precisely the
   count:0 ranges that `if (range.count > 0)` discarded. Three inputs taking
   three different branches produced one identical coverage set.
   The line expansion (each range widened to whole lines, then unioned) is a
   second, independent loss on top -- it re-merges branches that share a line,
   and most Set A targets ship a minified dist/index.js where a whole handler
   IS one line.

APPLIED: coverage_wrapper.js processCoverage() now calls executedIntervals(),
which subtracts nested count:0 ranges from each count>0 range and reports the
resulting disjoint intervals keyed by BYTE OFFSET (fileName|start|end), not by
line. Against the three measured range sets it yields 3 distinct coverage sets
instead of 1. 4 new tests in tests/test_audit_regressions.py, fixtures copied
verbatim from the measured ranges. Suite now 75 passed, 0 skipped.
*** MEASUREMENT CHANGE: this changes what cov: counts. Every coverage number
from here is BRANCH-INTERVAL coverage and is NOT comparable to any earlier
figure. ***

THE A/B/C, same config (api7/apisix-mcp::delete_resource), same seed, kept:
  A  eval/state/preRevertFix2/              Fix 2 active
       #13   INITED cov: 1388 ft: 1388 corp: 1/32b exec/s: 0
       #8192 pulse  cov: 1388 ft: 1388 corp: 1/32b exec/s: 107
     ft: == cov: exactly -- every slot triggered at most once, the suppression.
  B  eval/state/postRevertFix2-preBlockCov/ reverted, count>0 filter
       #13   INITED cov: 1390 ft: 2181 corp: 3/103b
       #355  REDUCE cov: 1390 ft: 2557 corp: 4/46b
       #4096 pulse  cov: 1390 ft: 2557 corp: 4/38b
     ft: and corp: move, cov: pinned. Revert was necessary but not sufficient.
  C  to be produced with executedIntervals().
Each directory has a WHY.md. Nothing was deleted.

