FULL AUDIT PASS -- 2026-09-10 08:00-09:00. SIX MORE BUGS, ALL MEASURED.
"Why did the last guy say it worked?" -- because nothing ever told him it
didn't. Every one of these reports a healthy "0 findings".
================================================================

BUG 6 -- V8 BLOCK COVERAGE: count>0 THROWS AWAY THE BRANCH INFORMATION
  Measured on one function, three branches, detailed:true:
      [routes  ] isBlockCoverage=true ranges= 157-265:1  207-263:0
      [services] isBlockCoverage=true ranges= 157-265:1  194-207:0  244-263:0
      [zzz     ] isBlockCoverage=true ranges= 157-265:1  194-207:0  231-244:0
  V8 emits ONE range for the whole function carrying its count, then carves the
  branches that did NOT run out of it as nested count:0 sub-ranges. The set of
  count>0 ranges is therefore IDENTICAL whichever branch runs. Three inputs,
  three branches, one coverage set.
  FIXED: processCoverage() -> executedIntervals(), which subtracts nested
  count:0 ranges and reports disjoint executed intervals keyed by BYTE OFFSET.
  Line expansion is gone too (it re-merged branches sharing a line, and most
  Set A targets ship a minified dist/index.js where a handler is one line).
  CONTROL EXPERIMENT (this is the proof, not the unit test): a synthetic stdio
  target with 6 branch-distinct inputs driven through the real JSRunner now
  yields 6 DISTINCT coverage sets. Before: 1.

BUG 7 -- /proc DOES NOT EXIST ON macOS, AND THE EVAL RUNS ON macOS
  fuzzer.py read os.listdir("/proc") in four places, each inside a bare
  `except Exception: pass`. Verified: FileNotFoundError [Errno 2] on
  Darwin-arm64. So before_pids and after_pids were ALWAYS empty, leaked_pids
  was ALWAYS empty, and check_process_and_fd_leaks / check_terminal_dos_and_leak
  could NEVER fire on the platform the campaign actually runs on. No error, no
  warning, just a sanitizer family that reports nothing forever.
  FIXED: _ps_snapshot() -> pid:(ppid,comm), /proc on Linux, `ps -Ao` elsewhere.
  Verified on this Mac: 435 pids, self found with correct ppid. If the snapshot
  ever comes back empty it now logs an ERROR instead of silently reporting clean.

BUG 8 -- EVERYTHING AFTER engine.fuzz() IS DEAD CODE
  libFuzzer terminates the process when -max_total_time expires, so the
  post-fuzz epilogue never runs. "[FUZZING METRICS]" and "Fuzzing session
  completed" appear in ZERO of every log this repo has ever produced -- and
  libFuzzer's own closing "Done ... runs" line is lost with them, because the
  stderr reader is a daemon thread and the pipe is not drained on a hard exit.
  Consequences: emit_final_snapshot() never ran (so the metrics/coverage CSVs
  are truncated), the corpus rmtree never ran, and any summary written there
  was never written at all.
  FIXED: the exec summary is emitted PERIODICALLY (first execution, then every
  512) instead of at the end; run_eval takes the LAST one.

BUG 9 -- "RAN AND FOUND NOTHING" WAS RECORDED IDENTICALLY TO "NEVER RAN"
  api7/apisix-mcp is an Admin-API client. Nothing listens on 127.0.0.1:9180, so
  EVERY input returns
      "Status: undefined\nMessage: connect ECONNREFUSED 127.0.0.1:9180"
  isError=true -> success=False -> _check_string_sanitizers returns early. No
  sanitizer can fire, coverage cannot vary, and all 32 apisix tools report a
  clean 0 findings. That is 32 of 173 configs, 18% of the campaign, producing
  authoritative-looking nothing.
  FIXED: fuzzer counts executions and successes and emits
      EXEC_SUMMARY tool=<t> n_exec=<n> n_success=<s> success_rate=<r>
      EXEC_ERROR n=<n> detail=<the recurring error>
      NOT_EXECUTABLE ... (once >=64 executions have all failed)
  run_eval records n_exec / n_exec_success / exec_success_rate / executable and
  SHOUTS in the summary. VERIFIED END TO END on a real smoke run:
      tool executability:      no=1, unknown=53
      !! 1 run(s) NEVER EXECUTED THE TOOL SUCCESSFULLY ... exclude them from
         any denominator ... 1 run(s): connect ECONNREFUSED 127.0.0.1:9180
      EXEC_SUMMARY tool=get_all_plugin_names n_exec=13312 n_success=0
  13312 executions, 0 successes, and the harness now says so instead of
  reporting a clean run.

BUG 10 -- THE DETERMINISTIC SEED MAPPER IGNORED "enum"
  _local_fallback_mapping never read enum and filled every string with "test".
  On mcp-appium-gestures::generate-double-tap-code, whose `language` is
  enum ['javascript','java'], 34 of 37 seeds carried language="test" and the
  server rejected EVERY ONE:
      MCP error -32602: Invalid ... parameters [{"received":"test", ...}]
  0 of 38 executions succeeded. Any tool with an enum-constrained required
  field was 100% unfuzzable through the deterministic path -- and the payloads
  in every OTHER field never reached the tool body either.
  input_parser._generate_value_for_schema ALREADY honoured enums, so the two
  paths disagreed with each other about the same schema.
  FIXED: defaults take a valid member; every member is exercised; exactly one
  out-of-enum probe is kept.
  MEASURED AFTER: 21/39 execute, 5 distinct coverage sets. Before: 0/38, 1.

BUG 11 -- THE ENTIRE FILE-BACKED PATH WAS DEAD IN THE EVAL HARNESS
  All 173 configs shipped predefined_seeds:[] and no file_backed_params, and
  src/__main__.py never even read that key -- so src/predefined_seeds/ (the 36
  files restored from b1b657e), FuzzFileManager and the "predefined_seed:<name>"
  protocol were unreachable from the eval, though targets/fuzz_*.py used them.
  A tool taking a file path received the literal string "test":
      MCP error -32600: File not found: test        (excel-reader, every input)
  THIS IS WHY THE PAPER'S BINARY-SEED CLAIM WAS NEVER OBSERVED WORKING.
  Nothing ever enabled it. See HANDOFF section 7.
  FIXED: __main__ reads and forwards file_backed_params; new
  eval/infer_file_params.py infers them from each tool's real schema
  (report-first, --apply required, --revert restores, per-config .bak written).
  23 configs updated.
  MEASURED AFTER, excel-reader::get_cell with sample.xlsx:
      cell='A1'      sheet='Sheet1'          success=True  cov=51
      cell='ZZ9999'  sheet='Sheet1'          success=True  cov=21
      cell='A1'      sheet='../../../etc/..' success=True  cov=26
      cell='=1+1'    sheet='Sheet1'          success=False cov=15
      distinct coverage sets: 4/4
  The tool executes for the first time. Note the third row: the sheet name
  '../../../etc/passwd' was ACCEPTED. Probably benign (sheet names are not
  paths) but it is exactly the shape of thing the oracle exists to judge --
  triage it, do not assume.

