BUGS 13 AND 14 -- 2026-09-10 09:00-09:20. THE TWO THAT MATTERED MOST.
Found by running the full pipeline on an EXECUTABLE target and reading
every number, instead of on apisix, which cannot execute at all.
================================================================

BUG 13 -- EVERY CORPUS ENTRY WAS SLICED INTO FRAGMENTS BEFORE PARSING
  fuzzer.py: sequence_steps = getattr(self.config, "sequence_steps", 2)
  FuzzerConfig has NO sequence_steps field, so the default of 2 ALWAYS won and
  sequence mode was permanently on. Measured on a real LLM-generated seed:
    original : {"filePath": "predefined_seed:test_file.xlsx", "sheetName": "Sheet1", "cell": "A1"}
      chunk 0: {"filePath": "predefined_seed:test_file.x        -> not JSON
      chunk 1: lsx", "sheetName": "Sheet1", "cell": "A1"        -> not JSON
      chunk 2: }                                                -> not JSON
  None of the three parses, so input_parser fell through to FuzzedDataProvider
  on every fragment:
      EXEC_ERROR n=1304 detail='MCP error -32600: Invalid cell address: '
      EXEC_SUMMARY tool=get_cell n_exec=512 n_success=0
  *** THIS SILENTLY DEFEATED audit Fix 3 (JSON-first parse order), the restored
  seed_bank/, LLM seed generation AND the new seed validation. All of those
  exist to deliver a COMPLETE argument object. A seed could be perfect and
  still never arrive. Fix 3 was correct and had no effect. ***
  FIXED: default 1. Sequence mode is opt-in via FuzzerConfig.sequence_steps.
  Splitting a serialised argument object at an arbitrary byte offset is not a
  sequence of tool calls; it is corruption. If stateful multi-call testing is
  wanted it needs its own representation.

BUG 14 -- EVERY ALERT FROM THE STRING PATH WAS A FALSE POSITIVE
  _check_string_sanitizers called EVERY selected sanitizer with a plain string,
  whatever its declared parameter type, then did `if triggered:` on the result.
  Sanitizers return either bool OR (bool, detail) -- and A 2-TUPLE IS ALWAYS
  TRUTHY, (False, '') INCLUDED. Measured against the live registry:
      check_file_access(...)                  called with a string -> (False, '')
      check_unhandled_promise_rejection(...)  called with a string -> (False, '')
      check_process_and_fd_leaks(leaked_pids: set[int])
          called with '/var/folders/.../tmp32djxuif.xlsx' ->
          (True, 'Leaked background subprocess PIDs detected ... <that path>')
          -- a non-empty string is a truthy "set of leaked pids".
  All three land in triggered_by: two via tuple-truthiness, one via a genuine
  type-confusion inside the sanitizer itself.
  ONE 90-SECOND RUN PRODUCED 295 ALERTS, ALL ONE SIGNATURE:
      ['check_file_access', 'check_unhandled_promise_rejection',
       'check_process_and_fd_leaks']
  on the benign input ToFu ITSELF constructed:
      {'filePath': '<temp>.xlsx', 'sheetName': 'Sheet1', 'cell': 'A1'}
  and the tool's output was correct. All three names are in
  BASE_MANDATORY_SANITIZERS. The LLM had selected
  ['check_path_traversal', 'check_file_access', 'check_resource_exhaustion']
  and NOT ONE LLM-attributable sanitizer fired.
  FIXED: tuple returns are unpacked before the truth test, and a sanitizer is
  only called on the string path if its first parameter is annotated `str` or
  unannotated (_takes_a_string, cached). 18 of the 71 registered sanitizers are
  eligible for the string path; the rest have their own dispatch. Deliberately
  permissive about UNannotated parameters -- most genuine string sanitizers
  predate the annotations and excluding them would silently disable the real
  checks, which is the precise failure this codebase keeps having.

  *** THIS ANSWERS HANDOFF OPEN DECISION 2 (nazmul.todo item 6). ***
  check_file_access / check_process_and_fd_leaks firing on a pure
  string-templating tool were NOT true positives and were NOT the Layer-1
  tracers catching the Node runtime's own I/O. They were this bug. The
  mandatory layer did not need a filter; the dispatch needed a type check.
  By extension every alert in eval/state/quarantine-pre-llmfix/ and
  quarantine-pre-parserfix/ (the 1390) and the 62 in the appium run carries
  this defect too. Quarantining them was right for a different reason than
  we thought.

- TO DOUBLE-CHECK (Ananto, do not erase until confirmed done):
   34. Item 6 / HANDOFF decision 2 can now be CLOSED, but confirm the reasoning
       yourself before you delete it -- it changes how the mandatory layer is
       described in the paper.
   35. _check_string_sanitizers still only inspects arguments where
       isinstance(val, str). With JSON-first parsing a seed can carry ints,
       bools, arrays and nested objects, and none of those is examined by any
       string sanitizer. Decide whether that is intended scope or a gap.
   36. _takes_a_string lets UNannotated sanitizers through. Annotate the real
       string sanitizers explicitly so the filter stops depending on absence of
       information, then tighten it to require `str`.

================================================================
G-TO-S COUPLING + FAIL-FAST -- 2026-09-10, Ananto signed off
================================================================

WHY: sec4_design.tex:10 -- "Tool metadata informs both seed generation and
sanitizer selection, ensuring that the property being exercised is also the
property used to judge the execution" -- and sec4_design.tex:158-163 --
"Values for the selected arguments are generated using the corresponding
sanitizer's adversarial input classes". Neither was implemented.
generate_seeds() never received the selected sanitizers, and
_local_fallback_mapping picked a seed-bank category by keyword match on the
ARGUMENT NAME. So the property exercised was chosen by one heuristic and the
property judged by a separate LLM call. Measured on excel-reader::get_cell:
the LLM selected check_path_traversal, the seeds were generic, and
check_path_traversal never fired. RQ4 asks exactly this and could not be
answered.

APPLIED (src/llm.py, src/fuzzer.py):
  - SANITIZER_SEED_CATEGORIES + categories_for_sanitizers(): maps a selected
    sanitizer to its adversarial input class, longest-fragment first.
  - generate_seeds(..., selected_sanitizers=...) -- the prompt now lists the
    selected sanitizers and offers ONLY their categories.
  - _local_fallback_mapping(..., selected_sanitizers=...) -- the argument name
    still decides WHICH argument carries the payload; the sanitizer decides
    WHAT payload.
  - fuzzer passes self.selected_sanitizer_names into seed generation.
  *** MEASUREMENT CHANGE: this changes what the fuzzer sends. Signed off. ***

FIRST REAL NUMBER OUT OF IT, and it is a paper result:
    Sanitizers selected: ['check_path_traversal','check_file_access','check_resource_exhaustion']
    SEEDGEN_CLASSES tool=get_cell categories=['path_traversal']
                    n_sanitizers_mapped=2 n_unmapped=9
    SEEDGEN_UNMAPPED n=9 sanitizers=['check_resource_exhaustion',
                    'check_unexpected_crash','check_introspection_...']
  11 active sanitizers (3 LLM-selected + the 10 always-on mandatory layer,
  minus overlap); only 2 have an adversarial input class. THE SEED BANK HAS 6
  CATEGORIES AGAINST 71 REGISTERED SANITIZERS, so most of any selection gets no
  generated inputs and its oracle can only fire incidentally. That fraction is
  now logged per tool (SEEDGEN_CLASSES / SEEDGEN_UNMAPPED) instead of being
  invisible. It belongs in RQ1 and it bounds RQ4.

FAIL-FAST (src/fuzzer.py): TOFU_FAILFAST_EXECS (default 2000). A tool with 0
successes after that many executions stops instead of burning the rest of the
budget. Threshold is deliberately generous -- the deterministic seeds alone
cannot execute excel-reader (they cannot guess a sheet name) while the LLM
seeds can, so a small sample would discard a target that actually works.
HONEST SAVING: a dead config costs ~450s, of which ~270s is the two gemma4
calls that happen BEFORE any execution. Fail-fast removes the ~180s fuzz tail,
not the LLM stage -- roughly 3h off a 143-dead-config campaign, not 9h. To
remove the LLM cost as well the executability probe would have to run before
seed generation, which risks discarding targets that only the LLM can drive.

PAPER MISMATCHES FOUND IN THE DESIGN-VS-CODE PASS (recorded, not all fixed --
Ananto's call is that results come first, but do not let these ship unchecked):
  1. FIXED: G-to-S coupling (above).
  2. OPEN: selection is per-TOOL; sec4_design.tex:79-82 defines S(a_i) per
     ARGUMENT. Consequence: a path sanitizer selected for a tool is applied to
     every string argument of that tool.
  3. OPEN: NO runtime instrumentation exists for JS targets.
     coverage_wrapper.js does coverage only -- no fs/child_process/http hooks.
     accessed_files is produced solely by python_runner.py:394, so for every
     Node target it is [] and every introspection oracle silently returns
     False. Table 3's 21 hooks are Python-only. Node is 1039/1847 runnable
     repos and the majority of Set A. Either add JS tracers or scope
     sec4.5.1 and RQ3 to Python explicitly.
  4. OPEN: the harness mutates SERIALISED BYTES; sec4_design.tex:147-150 says
     it operates on structured arguments precisely because "arbitrary
     byte-level mutations would frequently violate the invocation schema".
     EXEC_SUMMARY can now measure what fraction survives as schema-valid.
  5. OPEN: MCPInspector is a passthrough (name, description, inputSchema).
     sec4_design.tex:30 claims normalisation across MCP/LangChain/AutoGen/
     FastMCP, recursive leaf-level a_i descriptors, oneOf/anyOf/Union
     disjunctions and required/optional partitioning. None of it exists; only
     enum, and only in the input decoder and the seed mapper.
  6. OPEN: "alternating between partial invocations relying on defaults and
     fully specified adversarial payloads" -- the parser always fills every
     declared property. No partial-invocation path exists.
  7. NIT: paper says "~66 sanitizers"; the registry has 71.
  8. NIT: sec4_design.tex:212 says "Python's introspection library"; it is
     builtins monkey-patching, not sys.audit or inspect.

