FIRST CAMPAIGN WITH ALERTS -- 2026-09-10 13:22-14:48, 20 configs, 1.43h
ALL THREE HITS TRIAGED TO FALSE POSITIVES. TWO NEW DEFECTS.
================================================================

    HIT [2/20]  weread::get_book_best_reviews  334 alerts  1 sig  check_invalid_json
    HIT [5/20]  weread::search_books            70 alerts  1 sig  check_log_injection
    HIT [20/20] bing-cn::crawl_webpage          19 alerts  4 sigs url contract, resource
                                                                 exhaustion, process leak

These are the first alerts EVER produced by a pipeline that was verified
working, and all three signatures are LLM-ATTRIBUTABLE sanitizers rather than
the mandatory layer -- which is why they were worth taking seriously. They are
still false positives, for two new reasons.

BUG 16 -- A SANITIZER THAT CANNOT FIND ITS ARGUMENT FIRED ANYWAY
  check_strict_url_security_contract did generated_arguments.get("url").
  Written for targets/fuzz_http_check.py, whose argument IS called url; the LLM
  selects it for any URL-ish tool. crawl_webpage's argument is `urlMap`, so
  url_input was None and EVERY sub-check fired on None:
      Violations: [Raw Input Error: Expected str type, got NoneType |
        Structure Error: Missing scheme | Canonicalization Error: Hostname is
        empty | SSRF Check Error: Empty hostname].  Input: 'None'
  FIXED: _find_url_argument() locates the URL whatever it is called and handles
  the three real shapes -- plain string, list of strings, and MAP WHOSE VALUES
  are URLs. Returns False when the tool has no URL argument at all. A sanitizer
  that is not applicable must be SILENT; firing is a false positive that costs
  a reviewer's trust.
  *** GENERALISE THIS: every sanitizer in targets/fuzz_*.py was written against
  one hand-written harness with known argument names. Any of them that reads a
  fixed key has the same defect the moment the LLM selects it for a real tool.
  Audit the rest before the campaign that produces published numbers. ***

BUG 17 -- IN-BAND ERROR PAYLOADS COUNTED AS SUCCESSFUL EXECUTIONS
  mcp-server-weread has no cookie configured and answers EVERY input with a
  NORMAL MCP result whose payload is an error object, without setting isError:
      Tool Output : {'error': {'message': '没有找到cookie，请按照文档填写cookie或配置Cookie Cloud'}}
  So the runner scored every call a success. The tool reported
  n_exec=4096 n_success=4096 success_rate=1.0 executable=yes -- looking like
  the healthiest target in the campaign -- while never running its own logic,
  and the outcome oracle concluded it "executed successfully without raising"
  for inputs the sanitizers flagged. 404 of the 423 alerts are this.
  FIXED: JSRunner treats a top-level truthy `error`/`errors` member as a failed
  execution. Deliberately narrow -- {"error": null} and {"errors": []} are fine,
  and guessing at error text inside content blocks would trade one class of
  false positive for another. sec4_design.tex:270 already specifies this: the
  oracle evaluates "structured error types and schema rejections".

  *** THIS WIDENS THE ENVIRONMENT-EXCLUSION PROBLEM. *** weread passed the
  executability gate while being just as credential-blocked as graphlit. Any
  server that reports failure in-band was being counted as live. Re-measure the
  executability survey after this fix; the "6 of 14 repos execute" figure is
  optimistic.

WORTH RE-RUNNING, NOT DISMISSING
  With bug 16 fixed, urlMap {"u": "http://10.0.0.1/internal_api"} fires for the
  RIGHT reason, and the log shows crawl_webpage DID attempt that fetch,
  returning "抓取网页失败: 无法连接到服务器". A tool that accepts an RFC1918
  destination and tries to reach it is the SSRF shape ToFu exists to find. It
  failed only because nothing is listening on 10.0.0.1. Re-run it.

QUARANTINED: eval/state/quarantine-fp-20260910/ (3 records + 3 logs + WHY.md).
Active index is back to 74 runs, 0 alerts.

- TO DOUBLE-CHECK (Ananto, do not erase until confirmed done):
   39. check_resource_exhaustion fired at 5.01s against config.timeout_limit
       2.0s on a WEB-CRAWLING tool. A network fetch taking 5s is normal
       behaviour, not resource exhaustion. The limit has to be per-tool, or
       network-bound tools excluded from it, or the check is pure noise on any
       tool that makes an outbound request.
   40. check_process_and_fd_leaks reported "PID 60149 (node)" while TWO workers
       were running concurrently -- plausibly the other worker's MCP server.
       The ancestor filter in _execute_and_time uses the post-execution ps
       snapshot; verify it under --workers 2 before trusting any process-leak
       alert.
   41. Audit every sanitizer that reads a FIXED argument name (bug 16's family)
       before publishing numbers.

