THE SSRF QUESTION IS UNANSWERABLE ON A NODE TARGET, AND HERE IS THE PROOF
  crawl_webpage was fed http://192.168.1.1/admin, http://10.0.0.1/internal_api
  and http://169.254.169.254/latest/meta-data/. The URL contract stayed silent
  because is_input_rejected(res) saw the tool return an error, and counted that
  as a rejection. But the tool returned "抓取网页失败: 无法连接到服务器" --
  it ATTEMPTED the fetch and the connection was refused. "Refused to fetch"
  and "fetched and failed" are the difference between a correctly defended tool
  and an SSRF, and from the response alone they are indistinguishable.
  Telling them apart needs the NetworkExecution tracer, WHICH DOES NOT EXIST
  FOR JS (open mismatch 3). This is no longer an abstract gap: it is the reason
  a concrete SSRF candidate cannot be adjudicated. Node is the majority of the
  corpus.

CRASH CANDIDATE -- INVESTIGATED, NOT A FINDING
  The run exited with
      ==9038== ERROR: libFuzzer: fuzz target exited
      Test unit written to /tmp/crash-e05fe2e17ee75243e27eb5cec1051e5c3b594220
      {"u5ids": ["uuid_10"], "urlMap": {"uuid_10": "http://0.0.0.0:z0"}}
  Replayed that exact input against a live server: the process stayed ALIVE and
  returned a normal error. NOT REPRODUCIBLE, so it is not a target crash and
  must not be reported as one. Something else ended that process; the run also
  shows [JSRunner] Socket Communication Error: timed out elsewhere in the
  campaign. Diagnose before any crash count is published (item 43).

