--- ITEM 40 FIXED (2026-09-10): PROCESS-LEAK DETECTION WAS GLOBAL ---
_execute_and_time diffed a GLOBAL pid snapshot (set(ps_before) vs
set(ps_after)), so it saw every process the whole machine started during the
execution window. With --workers 2 that is the OTHER worker's MCP server:
    "Process leak/leftover detected: PID 38747 (node)"
reported on four nationalparks tools that spawn nothing. It fired in three
separate arms and produced most of the residual alert volume.

There WAS an ancestry filter and it is correct in isolation -- verified against
a synthetic ps map -- but it sat behind
    if leaked_pids and self.current_runner and self.current_runner.process:
so with no live `process` it was SKIPPED and the unfiltered global diff was
returned. IT FAILED OPEN. Same shape as every other bug in this file: the
degraded path was the permissive one.

FIXED: _descendants(root_pid, ps_map) walks the child map from the run's own
server pid, and BOTH snapshots are restricted to it before differencing. The
difference is therefore exactly "processes this tool call left behind". With no
root there are no descendants, so it now fails CLOSED. The ancestry re-filter is
gone as redundant.
4 tests: own-descendants only, other worker never attributed, no-root returns
empty, and a source check that a global pid set is never diffed again.
Suite: 109 passed.

STILL OPEN -- item 39 (check_resource_exhaustion, 2.0s limit vs tools that make
outbound HTTPS calls) is untouched: the threshold is a threat-model judgement,
not a bug. Expect nationalparks to keep alerting on it after this fix.

--- BUGS 18 + 19 VERIFIED, AND AN OVERCORRECTION CAUGHT (2026-09-10 18:32) ---
Re-ran the four false-positive configs on the gpt-4.1 arm:
    text_editor       426 alerts -> 0
    getAlerts          14 alerts -> 0
    getCampgrounds      2 alerts -> 0
    getEvents           2 alerts -> 0
    DONE 4 runs, 0 with alerts, 0 errors, 0 harness bugs
Bug 18 (structured refusal inside content[].text) and item 40 (global pid diff)
both confirmed fixed.

*** BUT THE BUG-18 FIX OVERCORRECTED `executable`. ***
Those tools are now marked executable=no:
    findParks          2560/2560 succ  yes
    getParkDetails     1024/1024       yes
    getVisitorCenters  1024/1024       yes
    getAlerts             0/1024       no   490x "Server error"
    text_editor           0/2000       no   428x "Path must be absolute"

text_editor WORKS. It rejected 2000 consecutive inputs because NOT ONE of
ToFu's mutated paths was absolute. That is a SEED-QUALITY defect in ToFu, not
an environment exclusion, and marking it "not executable" would silently drop a
working, security-relevant target out of the denominator -- the same
fail-quietly shape as everything else in this file, just pointing the other way.
It is also the same class as the enum bug: the input generator ignoring a
contract the tool states plainly ("Path must be absolute").

FIXED (reporting, not classification): parse_log records exec_error_kinds and
exec_error_top_share; summarize() now says a 0-success run is AMBIGUOUS and
prints both readings, with a stated heuristic rather than a silent verdict:
    (a) tool cannot run here      -> real environment exclusion
    (b) tool rejected OUR inputs  -> ToFu seed defect, KEEP in the denominator
    3 run(s): 'Server error'          [top error 64% across 5 kind(s)  -- varied, likely (b)]
    1 run(s): 'Path must be absolute' [top error 27% across 16 kind(s) -- varied, likely (b)]
One invariant message across wildly different inputs looks like (a); many
varied validation messages look like (b). The tool prints both and refuses to
decide. Do NOT let this heuristic become a silent classifier.

- TO DOUBLE-CHECK (Ananto, do not erase until confirmed done):
   48. SEED QUALITY IS NOW THE MEASURED BOTTLENECK ON WORKING TARGETS.
       text_editor: 0 of 2000 inputs were a valid absolute path.
       nationalparks getAlerts: 0 of 1024 succeeded while findParks on the SAME
       server succeeded 2560/2560. The fuzzer is generating inputs that never
       reach the tool's success path, so no sanitizer can observe real
       behaviour. This is a bigger limiter on findings than the sanitizer
       corpus. Root cause is the same family as the enum bug: byte-level
       mutation destroys contract-bearing fields (absolute paths, IDs, enum
       members) and the parser then rebuilds them from garbage.
       Candidate fix: structure-aware mutation that preserves a field's
       declared/inferred format, i.e. sec4_design.tex:147-150's actual claim.

================================================================================
2026-09-10  ADVERSARIAL REVIEW: Node code_exec instrumentation design  -> REJECT
================================================================================
Reviewed the proposed coverage_wrapper.js + js_runner.py code_exec hook
(inspector Debugger.scriptParsed + node:vm patch -> RunnerResult.evaluated_code_calls).
Everything below was MEASURED on Node v22.19.0, driving the PATCHED wrapper over
the real Unix-socket protocol with a Python client replicating js_runner.
Scratch artifacts: <scratchpad>/ce/{adv_target.mjs,adv_drive.py,probe1-6.mjs,covdrv.py}

WHAT HOLDS UP (verified, credit where due):
 - Not patching globalThis.eval is correct. `function calc(e){const secret=42;
   return eval(e)} calc("secret + 1")` returned 43 through the instrument.
   Patching it demotes direct eval to indirect eval -> ReferenceError. Keep this.
 - Debugger.scriptParsed carries params.stackTrace by DEFAULT (no
   setAsyncCallStackDepth needed), and session.post('Debugger.getScriptSource')
   invoked from inside the notification handler DOES call back synchronously.
   Both are load-bearing assumptions and both are true.
 - scriptFailedToParse fires for eval('this is (((not js') and its source is
   retrievable. Real gap, correctly closed.
 - vm patch via process.getBuiltinModule('node:module').createRequire does
   propagate to a target's `import { runInNewContext } from 'node:vm'`
   (ns.runInNewContext === patched -> true). The facade-snapshot reasoning is right.
 - Module.prototype._compile disk-compare produced NO false positive on shebang
   or BOM .cjs files.
 - Element dict shape {"function":str,"expression":str} matches
   check_introspection_code_injection (introspection.py:487-488) exactly; the
   oracle raises AttributeError on a bare string, so js_runner's _sane_code_calls
   coercion is required and sufficient. Target tool results were unchanged.

WHY IT IS REJECTED ANYWAY -- three measured defects, all of the HANDOFF.md §0 kind:

 1. SILENT MISS, PAYLOAD-CONTROLLABLE. A `//# sourceURL=` comment in the
    evaluated source sets scriptParsed's `url`, and all three of the block's
    bail-outs then drop the record (block.js:123-127).
      eval(payload)                                -> recorded
      eval(payload + '\n//# sourceURL=payload.js') -> NOT recorded, eval still ran
      eval(payload + '\n//# sourceURL=file:///tmp/p.js') -> NOT recorded
    Any target that appends a sourceURL for debugging (common in template
    engines / `eval(code + '//# sourceURL=' + name)`) makes the WHOLE family
    report zero for that target, forever, with a healthy-looking tofu_diag.

 2. MANUFACTURED FINDING ON LAZY INIT. check_introspection_code_injection fires
    on the PRESENCE of any record. The novelty baseline only covers compiles
    that happen BEFORE the first tools/call. Most MCP servers lazy-init. A
    target caching `new Function(...)` on first call:
      call 1 (lazy_init) -> fires=True, function='new Function'   <-- FP
      call 2 (lazy_init) -> fires=False
    _handle_violation reports and sets silent_bug_found. The design's own noise
    test compiled its validator at STARTUP, which is exactly the case this misses.

 3. ORDER-DEPENDENT ORACLE. Identical input, twice:
      call 8 (direct_eval "1+1") -> fires=True
      call 9 (direct_eval "1+1") -> fires=False (suppressedNovel:1)
    The eval genuinely ran both times. The attribute stops meaning "code was
    evaluated during this execution" -- which is what the oracle's alert text
    asserts and what PythonRunner's evaluated_code_calls means. Python-vs-JS
    arm numbers are then not comparable, and tofuSeen.clear() at 4096 makes
    the suppression depend on unrelated compiles.

MEASUREMENT METHODOLOGY OF THE DESIGN IS ITSELF WRONG (both guards measure the
wrong quantity -- flagging because this is the recurring failure):
 - "throughput within ~5% (-2.6/-4.0/-3.1%)": measured on a target that never
   compiles code, where Debugger.enable costs NOTHING (no-eval loop: 196ms vs
   197ms, i.e. 0%). On an eval-per-call target the real cost is:
     end-to-end over the socket: 1737 -> 1495 calls/s  (-14%, 2 trials)
     microbench:  426k -> 75k evals/s  (6.3x slower, stable across N)
   check_resource_exhaustion is in BASE_MANDATORY_SANITIZERS, so a 6x slowdown
   on eval-heavy targets can manufacture a timeout finding.
 - "coverage identity unchanged": the guard compared the FILE-NAME SET. Split by
   file, target coverage really is identical (11 == 11 entries, sets equal) --
   but wrapper self-coverage went 47 -> 101 entries. That only bites when no
   package.json is found (targetDir degenerates to '/', coverage_wrapper.js:176-182),
   in which case the patch doubles the junk fed to Atheris as target coverage.
 - Debugger.enable also retains parsed scripts: post-gc RSS over 100k evals
   grew +11.7MB with it off vs +34.6MB with it on. A long eval-heavy campaign
   will grow monotonically.

OTHER BLIND SPOTS (not necessarily disqualifying, must be documented if adopted):
 - Any compile AFTER the first stdout chunk is missed -- tofuCollecting is
   cleared there. Verified: a tool that writes its response then evals 5ms later
   -> fires=False. Also inherits the pre-existing split-response truncation bug.
 - Worker threads and child processes (acknowledged by the design).
 - WebAssembly.compile/instantiate emits no scriptParsed -- arbitrary code
   execution with zero records.
 - vm.SourceTextModule (--experimental-vm-modules) and module.register()/custom
   ESM loader hooks are not covered.
 - js_runner early returns at :206 and :226 carry no tofu_js_diag, so a
   socket-error run reports no diagnostics -- a silent-zero hole in the very
   field added to prevent silent zeros.

CONDITIONS FOR RE-REVIEW (all four):
 a. Do not bail on a non-empty scriptParsed url. Distinguish a REAL module load
    from a sourceURL-relabelled eval by the stack frame + embedderName, not by
    the url string.
 b. Kill the cross-call novelty set, or move dedup to the Python side where it
    is visible and per-execution. The oracle must stay a pure function of the
    execution.
 c. Baseline the framework by call index (e.g. ignore records from call #1, or
    require a site to be attacker-reachable), not by "before the first call".
 d. Re-run the CLAUDE.md §4 acceptance test against a target that ACTUALLY
    compiles code per call, and compare coverage entry sets per file, not
    file-name sets.

TO DOUBLE-CHECK: whether SECURITY_PROFILES["read_only"] (sanitizers/__init__.py:200-208)
can force check_introspection_code_injection on. It lists that sanitizer, but I
found no reference to SECURITY_PROFILES in fuzzer.py or llm.py -- if it is wired
in elsewhere, this family becomes a third always-on layer (attribution trap,
HANDOFF.md §2) rather than the LLM-selection-gated one it looks like at
fuzzer.py:768.

================================================================
JS RUNTIME INSTRUMENTATION -- DESIGN PASS, 2026-09-10 (15 agents,
2.1M subagent tokens, 85 evidenced facts). NOTHING SHIPPED, ON PURPOSE.
================================================================

Six hook families designed independently (fs, child_process, network,
code_exec, crypto, sql_and_data), each adversarially reviewed by an agent told
to assume the design is WRONG and to execute the real oracles against the
proposed records. Result:

    contract_matches: FALSE on ALL SIX.
    4 REJECT, 2 ADOPT_WITH_CHANGES, and network additionally
    will_break_target: TRUE.

*** THE SHARED BLOCKER (5 of 6 designs) -- FIX THIS BEFORE ANY HOOK ***
Every design adds its data to the wrapper's socket payload. NONE of them edits
js_runner.py. src/runners/js_runner.py:231-241 reads exactly two keys --
    cov_data  = payload.get("coverage", [])
    resp_str  = payload.get("response", "")
-- inside a bare `except Exception: pass`, and builds RunnerResult at :329-335
with five fields. Anything else is JSON-serialised, sent over the socket, and
DROPPED ON THE FLOOR; every getattr(res, ...) in fuzzer.py then returns its []
default. Verified: grep for accessed_files|written_files|res_obj|setattr in
js_runner.py returns NOTHING.
=> As specified, all six families would have shipped a perfectly-shaped
   instrument that reports 0 findings while looking healthy. HANDOFF section 0,
   verbatim, for the fifth time. The integration point is the bug, not the hooks.

PER-FAMILY DEFECTS, each MEASURED by running the real oracle on the real record:
  child_process  emits "command"; check_introspection_unnecessary_root_privileges
                 indexes cmd.get("cmd", "") at introspection.py:2024 -> CWE-250/272
                 silently dead.
  sql_and_data   `params: null` overloads two meanings; introspection.py:308 reads
                 `params is None or len(params)==0` as UNPARAMETERISED -> false
                 positives on correctly parameterised queries.
  fs             written_files["mode"] is an open-mode STRING ("w"); 
                 check_introspection_insecure_file_permissions (:1976) needs an
                 int and falls back to os.stat IN THE FUZZER PROCESS. chmod's
                 permission argument is discarded entirely -- the exact CWE-276/732
                 signal. Delete handlers omit the underTmp() guard recWrite has, so
                 ANY failed delete of any still-existing path manufactures
                 CWE-533 + CWE-459. Measured: fs.rmSync('./pkg') on a directory ->
                 leaked_temp_files ["./pkg"] -> two findings.
  code_exec      per-element shape correct; LIST SEMANTICS wrong.
  crypto         shapes verified correct against three real oracles; other defects.
  network        shapes correct AND THE TRACER ALTERS TARGET BEHAVIOUR:
                 the dns.lookup hook rebuilds the function without copying
                 Symbol(customPromisifyArgs), so promisify(dns.lookup) changes
                 semantics. Worse, extractAddress defaults an absent host to
                 'localhost', which is in check_introspection_ssrf's
                 internal_host_patterns (:567) -- a benign target doing
                 net.connect({port:6379}) to a local Redis would emit a
                 GUARANTEED SSRF ALERT ON EVERY EXECUTION. It would have
                 manufactured the finding we most want.

BLIND SPOTS COMMON TO EVERY FAMILY (measured, not assumed):
  child processes  execSync('cat /etc/passwd') -> accessed_files []
  worker threads   new Worker(...,{eval:true}) reading /etc/passwd -> []
                   (compounded: coverage_wrapper.js:35 stores workerSessions
                    that :117-121 never polls, so worker COVERAGE is discarded too)
  native addons    better-sqlite3, sharp, esbuild -- N-API I/O in C++ bypasses
                   the JS exports entirely
These are the same exclusion class as "ToFu ships two runners". State them.

ONE VERIFIER CAUGHT A FABRICATED JUSTIFICATION, worth recording as process:
a design justified an edit with "getLineOffsets runs inside processCoverage, so
without this the target's source files land in accessed_files on every call".
VERIFIED FALSE: getLineOffsets/offsetToLine are defined at :139/:158 and CALLED
NOWHERE -- the byte-offset rewrite at :235-273 made them dead code. The edit is
a harmless no-op with a measured-sounding rationale attached. That is exactly
the CLAUDE.md rule-1 pattern, produced by a model, caught by an adversarial
reader. Keep the adversarial stage.

ORDER OF WORK WHEN THIS IS BUILT (not started):
  1. js_runner.py payload plumbing FIRST, with a test that fails if a known key
     does not reach RunnerResult. Without it every hook is decorative.
  2. Then ONE family end to end, with a control target, before the next.
     Start with network -- it is what makes an SSRF verdict possible -- but only
     after the dns.lookup symbol copy and the 'localhost' default are fixed.
  3. Never land a family whose verifier says contract_matches:false.

- TO DOUBLE-CHECK (Ananto, do not erase until confirmed done):
   49. The full designs + verdicts are in the workflow journal:
       .claude/projects/.../subagents/workflows/wf_a107e419-5ce/journal.jsonl
       They contain runnable code. Nothing from them is in the repo yet.
   50. Decide whether JS instrumentation is in scope for THIS paper at all. If
       not, sec4.5.1 / Table 3 / RQ3 must be scoped to Python explicitly and the
       Node majority reported as a stated limitation -- which is defensible, and
       cheaper than six families done properly.

