2026-09-11  DEFENSECLAW (Benchmarks/defenseclaw) -- RUNNABILITY ASSESSMENT
================================================================
Ananto asked if defenseclaw (another Cisco AI Defense product) is runnable.
Repo: github.com/cisco-ai-defense/defenseclaw @ 27cafb9. Go+Python governance
tool for "OpenClaw and agentic AI runtimes": scan capabilities, inspect runtime
traffic, export audit evidence.

VERDICT: NOT runnable as-is, and adds ~nothing over the MCP Scanner we already
fixed. Do not invest unless we specifically want the runtime-governance angle.

WHY not runnable now:
  - No Go toolchain installed. go.mod pins `go 1.26.4` (a minimum directive).
    brew go is 1.27.1 -> would satisfy it, but installing is a user action.
  - `make build` = THREE components: Python CLI (have py3.11 OK), Go gateway
    (needs go), and an OpenClaw plugin. Multi-toolchain build.
  - No prebuilt binary vendored.

WHY not worth it for the baseline:
  - Its LOCAL MCP scan just WRAPS the same open-source mcp-scanner binary we
    already patched and got working (config.py:897 `binary: str="mcp-scanner"`,
    bootstrap.py:1532 "defenseclaw setup mcp-scanner"). Same detections, extra
    layers. No new static-analysis capability for the ToFu comparison.
  - Its differentiated value (remote scan, guardrails, runtime traffic
    inspection) is scanner_mode remote/both -> Cisco AI Defense CLOUD
    (cisco_api_key_env=CISCO_AI_DEFENSE_API_KEY, cisco_endpoint), i.e. the same
    paid subscription the MCP Scanner's `api` analyzer needs. We don't have it.
  - It targets OpenClaw runtimes, not standalone MCP servers over stdio; wiring
    it to ToFu's prepared.jsonl targets would be new integration work for a
    result equivalent to the mcp-scanner baseline (item 61).

RECOMMENDATION: for Table 2's static-analysis baseline, use the MCP Scanner
directly (already working, item 61). Cite defenseclaw as the productized
wrapper and note the runtime/cloud features are out of scope (no subscription).
Only build defenseclaw if a reviewer demands the runtime-governance comparison.
   62. (only if needed) Build defenseclaw: brew install go; `make build`;
       point its local mcp-scanner at prepared.jsonl. Expect parity with the
       mcp-scanner baseline, so low priority.

